# Take the font list away from window.queryLocalFonts().
#
# The Local Font Access API returns every font installed on the machine in one
# call. It is the strongest font signal there is and the only one that does not
# have to be measured a family at a time, and a fingerprinting script that gets
# it does not need any of the measurement tricks. Firefox and Safari do not
# implement it. Upstream does not ship it on Android - the status field it had
# was {"Android": "", "default": "stable"}. So the desktop builds were the
# outlier among their peers and among their own siblings, which is the same
# shape of gap default-flags.patch just closed for the canvas and WebGL work.
#
# It is permission-gated, and that is not the defence it sounds like. The
# prompt is a single click, it is asked at the moment a design tool needs an
# answer, and granting it hands over the whole list at once - there is no
# partial answer to give.
#
# Off by default with a flag to turn it back on, rather than removed, because
# there is a real use: browser-based design tools offer your own fonts through
# this API and fall back to web fonts without it. Setting status to "" gives
# the generated blink::features::kFontAccess a disabled default, and the json5
# schema's copied_from_base_feature_if defaults to "enabled_or_overridden", so
# a chrome://flags override to Enabled turns the Blink feature back on. That is
# the mechanism the entry relies on; it is not a decorative flag.
#
# Deliberately not done here: Cromite's Fonts-fingerprinting-mitigation.patch,
# which is the other half of this problem - the measurement channel, where a
# page renders text in a candidate family and compares widths. That one is not
# portable to this project as written, for three reasons, all read out of the
# patch rather than assumed:
#
#   * Its allowlist, bromite_allowed_fonts.h, is the Windows 11 font list plus
#     nine general names and an Android section. There is no Linux section at
#     all, so on a Linux build IsFontAllowed() would reject essentially every
#     real family and fall back - it would not harden the browser, it would
#     break text on it.
#   * Its Windows half reaches into private Skia internals - it includes
#     src/ports/SkTypeface_win_dw.h and reads DWriteFontTypeface::fDWriteFont
#     and fDWriteFontFamily directly - which is a rebase hazard on every Skia
#     roll, in a file we would then own.
#   * It changes FontCache::CreateTypeface's signature and disables
#     AlternateFontName::kLocalUniqueFace, and its Android counterpart is 3300
#     lines.
#
# The measurement channel therefore stays open, and it is better to say that
# plainly than to ship a partial version of it that renders text wrong. It
# wants its own pass with an allowlist built for the platforms this project
# actually ships.
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
@@ -3268,8 +3268,17 @@
       status: "stable",
     },
     {
+      // Aerium: off, which is what Android already gets. This is
+      // window.queryLocalFonts() - the Local Font Access API - and it hands a
+      // page the complete list of fonts installed on the machine in one call,
+      // which is the strongest font signal there is and the only one that does
+      // not have to be measured a family at a time. Firefox and Safari do not
+      // implement it and upstream does not ship it on Android, so the desktop
+      // builds were the outlier among both their peers and their own siblings.
+      // Turn it back on at chrome://flags/#aerium-local-font-access; the entry
+      // exists because design tools are a real use for it.
       name: "FontAccess",
-      status: {"Android": "", "default": "stable"},
+      status: "",
     },
     {
       name: "FontDataServiceForCSSLocalFonts",
--- a/chrome/browser/aerium_flag_entries.h
+++ b/chrome/browser/aerium_flag_entries.h
@@ -25,4 +25,13 @@
      "changes that hash without changing anything you can hear, and none of "
      "these paths feeds playback. On by default. Aerium flag.",
      kOsAll, FEATURE_VALUE_TYPE(blink::features::kAeriumAudioNoise)},
+    {"aerium-local-font-access",
+     "Local Font Access API",
+     "Let sites call window.queryLocalFonts() to read the list of fonts "
+     "installed on this computer, after asking permission. Off in Aerium: the "
+     "list is the strongest font fingerprint there is, and one prompt hands "
+     "over all of it. Firefox and Safari do not offer this API at all, and "
+     "Chrome does not offer it on Android. Turn it on if you use a design tool "
+     "in the browser that needs your fonts. Aerium flag.",
+     kOsAll, FEATURE_VALUE_TYPE(blink::features::kFontAccess)},
 #endif  // CHROME_BROWSER_AERIUM_FLAG_ENTRIES_H_
